Original repository path: README.md

# Tiny Image Star

Tiny Image Star is a private, browser-based photo story and image editor.
Compose a set of photos into editable slides, or frame, resize and convert
individual images and large collections. Images stay on your
device: there is no account, upload service, analytics endpoint, or application
backend.

> **Pre-release status:** the current export formats are PNG and fixed-setting JPEG. Additional
> formats appear only after the checked-in image engine proves that it can
> produce valid output bytes. The project does not yet have a chosen software
> license, so reuse rights remain pending; see [License status](#license-status).

## What works today

- **Make a story:** assemble 6–12 photos into 4–8 editable slides, with
  own-photo look previews, adaptive portrait/tall layouts, captions, crop zoom,
  per-shape positions, local adjustments and ordered
  PNG/JPEG exports. Choose one or both shapes, pause preparation, and retry
  failed files while keeping ready outputs. See [story scope](docs/STORY_WORKSPACE_VERIFICATION.md)
  and [export behavior](docs/STORY_EXPORT_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Story recipes:** Scrapbook, Depth cover and Film diary add distinct cover,
  detail and closing treatments. Tape and film details follow their photos;
  Depth cover requires a selected cover subject. The original color-only looks
  remain available. See [recipe behavior and qualification limits](docs/STORY_RECIPE_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Bundled story fonts:** recipe revision two uses four pinned, licensed fonts
  served from this site and retained with saved stories. Shared styles carry
  verified font references. Device fonts remain an explicit alternative;
  see [typography behavior and current limits](docs/STORY_FONT_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Smaller story sources:** opt into 2,048px editing copies for large photos
  while keeping originals in the saved story. Photos also converts existing
  edited sources and their cutouts together, with Apply/Cancel and undo.
  Restoring the original keeps current edits; upscaled masks cannot regain lost
  edge detail. Exports use the selected source.
  See [memory and quality limits](docs/WORKING_COPY_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Reusable story styles:** favorites, recent choices, named styles with
  Look/Text/Layout/Output, cutout finish and depth-title choices, and
  parameter-only style file sharing. Depth styles preserve your words and
  subject selections while reusing type, placement and background settings.
  See [format, storage limits and remaining work](docs/STYLE_LIBRARY_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Photo looks for image sets and folders:** preview the style's photo colors
  on your own image, adjust strength, and apply to all, selected or one image.
  Saved looks retain their version through recovery and folder processing.
  See [photo look scope and evidence](docs/PHOTO_LOOK_EXECUTION_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Manual cutouts in stories:** import a matching PNG mask or use soft Restore
  and Erase brushes. Inspect source pixels at 100–400%, repair individual pixels,
  undo and recover the edit locally.
  See [cutout controls and current limits](docs/MASK_EDITING_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Depth titles:** place editable words behind a manually selected subject,
  keep the original photo or use the cutout over the page, and adapt the title
  with its photo across output shapes. Replacing a photo keeps the words while
  you choose its new subject.
  See [rendering, phone controls and current limits](docs/DEPTH_TITLE_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Connected cutouts:** copy a manually selected subject across two slides,
  review both halves, and keep the joined slides together when reordering.
  Position and size adapt separately to each output shape.
  See [controls, seam checks and current limits](docs/CONNECTED_CUTOUT_VERIFICATION.md).
- **Cutout finishes:** add a colored outline and soft shadow, fine-tune their
  placement, and save the treatment as a reusable style. Finishes follow subject
  masks, including connected copies and depth titles.
  See [rendering and current limits](docs/CUTOUT_EFFECTS_VERIFICATION.md).
- One canvas for one image or an image set—there is no separate batch editor.
- Pan, zoom, visual crop handles, aspect presets, fit or fill resizing,
  rotate, flip, brightness, contrast, and grayscale.
- Add, move, resize, and style text directly on the canvas. Use built-in fonts
  or drop a local font file; text is included in Pillow-encoded PNG/JPEG output
  for individual images, image sets and large-folder jobs.
- Immediate original/edited comparison, undo, redo, reset, and dirty-state
  feedback.
- Light, Dark, and System appearance, remembered locally and applied before the
  first paint.
- Destination-based recipes, custom local presets, per-image corrections, and
  explicit apply-to-all/selected/this-image scope.
- Worker-backed processing, responsive result selection, direct single-image
  save, and folder-based multi-image save where the browser supports it.
- A bounded-memory large-folder path with pause, resume, and retry. It avoids
  retaining the entire collection or creating thousands of preview cards.
- Browser-local recovery for active work and a visible control for clearing
  saved recipes, custom fonts, and recovery data.

The detailed implemented/blocked boundary is in
[the verification matrix](VERIFICATION_MATRIX.md). In particular, AVIF,
animation preservation, and exports beyond PNG/JPEG are not currently claimed.
JPEG flattens transparent areas onto white; PNG preserves transparency. The
pinned encoder has no quality option, so quality controls remain hidden.

## Run it locally

Tiny Image Star is a static site. From the repository root, start any local
HTTP server; Python's built-in server is enough:

```bash
python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1
```

Open <http://127.0.0.1:8000/>. Do not open `index.html` through a `file://` URL:
browser workers and the image engine require an HTTP origin.

For a random free port, replace `8000` with `0` and open the port printed by
Python.

## First image in four steps

1. Choose, drop, or paste one or more images.
2. Drag the image or crop frame and choose a familiar destination or shape.
3. Compare the original with the edited result and apply the change to this
   image, selected images, or all images.
4. Save the selected result. Multiple results use a folder when the browser
   offers direct folder access; otherwise the app presents an explicit
   one-file-at-a-time save queue.

Presets and recovery copies are stored only in this browser. A per-image
correction remains separate from the shared recipe, so later set-wide changes
do not silently erase it.
Saved-recipe edits commit atomically. If another tab changes the same recipe,
your draft stays open with an option to save a new copy. Existing applied
copies keep their settings; see [migration details](docs/LEGACY_PRESET_MIGRATION.md).
An image set can use different versions of one recipe on different images.
Version labels identify each choice, and saved recovery copies retain those
exact versions even after you change or delete the library entry.

Open **Photo look** in the image tray, Results or the phone's **Batch** sheet.
Choose a look and strength, preview it, then apply it to the intended images.
**Undo look change** restores the preceding look without removing unrelated
corrections. Photo colors stay separate from your crop, text and destination;
story layouts and typography are available in the story editor.

Processing offers Auto, Max speed, and Low resource. Editor/story previews,
look thumbnails, exports, batches and folder jobs share CPU and estimated memory admission, with priority for
the current image and reusable workers. Worker counts above four are supported
where the budget permits; real-device speed and sustained-memory qualification
remain pending. See [scheduler evidence](docs/SHARED_SCHEDULER_VERIFICATION.md).
When Web Locks are available, tabs in the same browser storage partition also
share CPU and estimated memory capacity. Processing in one tab can wait while
another is busy; closing that tab releases its reservations through the browser.

## Large folders

The large-folder path is shown only when the browser provides direct folder
read/write access and Web Locks. It discovers supported files into an IndexedDB metadata
manifest, processes a bounded number in parallel, and writes completed files
straight to the selected destination.

Choose the source folder, then the recipe and optional **Photo look**. Preview
the look on the first source photo before applying it. Finally choose the save
folder and start processing. Selecting the save folder fixes the job's recipe
and look, so every file uses the same copied settings even if the library changes.

One tab owns each saved job. A journal verifies completed files after an
interrupted save and prevents duplicate completion counts; conflicting files
are preserved for review. See [recovery evidence and limits](docs/FOLDER_RECOVERY_VERIFICATION.md).

The code and regression suite model up to 100,000 manifest entries without
retaining source or output bytes for the collection. That is a design and
automated-contract result—not a claim that every browser/device has completed
a 100,000-image production run. See [the large-folder plan](LARGE_FOLDER_PLAN.md)
for memory boundaries, recovery semantics, and the dated benchmark method.

## Privacy and local data

Edits now use versioned project records and bounded command history. Recovery
stores source assets separately, verifies their hashes, and preserves legacy
records during migration. **Local data → Back up saved work** downloads a private
recovery archive including sources, recipes and fonts. Archive import remains
in development. See [project evidence and limits](docs/PROJECT_MODEL_VERIFICATION.md).

The application sends no image-processing, telemetry or analytics requests.
The static host receives normal requests for HTML, JavaScript, CSS, image-engine
files and the bundled story fonts. Fonts load only from this site's pinned
asset pack, without a third-party font service. Saved stories retain their font
bytes locally; device fonts are an explicit option without a font download.

Local state may include:

- original recipes in browser local storage, plus immutable migrated recipes
  and reusable story styles in IndexedDB;
- active-session source bytes and operation metadata in IndexedDB, within the
  documented recovery budget; and
- large-folder manifest metadata plus browser-granted directory handles.
- locally added font files in the browser's font store.

Use **Local data** in the application footer to inspect and clear saved recipes,
custom font files, and recovery records. Fonts already loaded into the current
page can remain usable until refresh; downloaded files and files written to a
chosen output folder are outside that browser-local cleanup.

## Browser and format boundaries

The app needs JavaScript modules, Web Workers, WebAssembly, Canvas, Blob/Object
URL support, and IndexedDB. It detects optional capabilities at runtime instead
of inferring them from a browser name. Direct large-folder input/output needs a
browser-provided directory picker; the smaller one-or-many workflow remains
available when that feature is absent.

Verified still-image inputs have fixtures for JPEG, PNG, GIF, BMP, WebP, TIFF,
ICO, and EXIF-oriented JPEG. Animated GIF/WebP is rejected explicitly because
animation preservation is not implemented. SVG, HEIC/HEIF, JPEG XL, PSD, PDF,
camera RAW, and AVIF are outside the current verified contract.

Text is shaped locally in a worker and composited before Pillow's final
PNG/JPEG encode. The shared scheduler reserves memory for text surfaces and
referenced font bytes. Missing or corrupt custom fonts produce an explicit
error. A Google Fonts page is a discovery link: download a font file, then drop
it into the editor. See [text composition evidence](docs/TEXT_COMPOSITOR_VERIFICATION.md)
for concurrency checks, font limits and remaining typography/device gates.

## Development and verification

Contributors need Node.js 20 or newer. Install the locked development
dependency and the Chromium browser used by the smoke suite. GNU Make 3.81+
is an optional convenience interface over the same npm scripts:

```bash
make install
make setup-browser
```

Run the complete local/CI gate:

```bash
make verify
```

Useful narrower commands:

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `npm test` | Fast deterministic checks using real fixture bytes. |
| `npm run verify:watch` | Rerun the fast checks after relevant files change. |
| `npm run verify:browser` | Browser interactions, responsive layout, output bytes, and console errors. |
| `npm run verify:scheduler` | Shared admission, worker lifecycle, failure, fairness, cancellation, and Auto policy checks. |
| `npm run verify:project-model` | Project validation, stable geometry, overrides, command history and dependency invalidation. |
| `npm run verify:folder-recovery` | Concurrent tabs, durable claims/counters, terminated writers, recovery and file-conflict protection. |
| `npm run migration:benchmark` | Run the PNG regression and long-running, pinned browser collection matrix with isolated journaled test writes; see the [method and results](docs/COLLECTION_BENCHMARKS.md). |
| `npm run check:docs` | Check repository-local Markdown links. |
| `npm run check:pages -- _site` | Validate an already assembled Pages directory. |
| `npm run profile:folder -- /path/to/images 64` | Profile an evenly distributed local sample without retaining benchmark outputs. |
| `make verify-fast` / `make verify-browser` | Make aliases for the two verification layers. |
| `make package-pages` | Build, optimize, Brotli-check, and validate the exact Pages artifact in `_site/`. |

Run `make help` for the complete contributor command list. `make package-pages`
is a local filesystem mutation; `make verify`, `npm test`, and the two verify
layers are read-only apart from normal temporary test data.

`npm run verify:all` returns nonzero if either layer fails; it does not silently
skip the browser test. `make package-pages` is the release-style build: it
minifies app-owned JavaScript and CSS and creates Brotli sidecars. Published
Pillow JS/WASM stay unchanged and their hashes are verified. The app can consume the Brotli
WASM sidecar locally when the browser supports it, while retaining the normal
WASM loader as a compatibility fallback.

The release build uses identifier minification to reduce bytes; it is not
security obfuscation and cannot hide browser-delivered source code.
The checked artifact contains `index.html`, minified `styles.css`, `src/`, and
`wasm/` files plus `.br` validation/consumption sidecars. GitHub Pages does
not provide a project-controlled `Content-Encoding` header, so the JavaScript
and CSS sidecars are retained for verifiable/precompressed hosting rather than
claimed as automatically served by Pages. [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)
explains repository structure, generated files, and pull-request expectations.

GitHub Actions runs the same complete gate on Node.js 20 and 24 for pull
requests and before a main branch deployment. It then assembles, validates,
and browser-smoke-tests the optimized Pages artifact on every event;
deployment is enabled only for non-PR events. See [RELEASING.md](RELEASING.md)
and [MAINTAINERS.md](MAINTAINERS.md) for the maintainer workflow.

